Original commit message:

    Reland "[regexp] Fix IS_CERTAINLY_ONE_CODE_POINT for unicode-mode classes"

    This is a reland of commit 2f7f93cf5c1035086dcdf9758b2e604adab44ca6

    Reland: Test skipped in 'not i18n'.

    Original change's description:
    > [regexp] Fix IS_CERTAINLY_ONE_CODE_POINT for unicode-mode classes
    >
    > ParseCharacterClass marked every non-negated class in a one-byte pattern
    > source as IS_CERTAINLY_ONE_CODE_POINT. But under /u (and /iu) a one-byte
    > source can still describe supplementary code points, e.g. \u{1F600},
    > \uD83D\uDE00, \p{...}, or the /iu case closure of [ß] once Unicode 18
    > adds U+1DF95. Such a class consumes a surrogate pair, i.e. two code
    > units, so max_match() returned 1 instead of 2.
    >
    > The end-anchor optimization in regexp.cc uses max_match() to position
    > the start of the search from the end of the subject. With the
    > underestimate, matching started too close to the end and valid matches
    > were silently missed, e.g. /x[\u{1F600}]$/u.test('x\u{1F600}') returned
    > false.
    >
    > Gate the flag on !IsUnicodeMode() instead of pattern width. Outside of
    > unicode mode a class always matches exactly one code unit, regardless of
    > source width or negation, so the flag can now also be set for negated
    > classes and two-byte patterns there.
    >
    > Fixed: 571871980
